$ grep -rn "api.codedev.test/courses" src/
src/features/catalog/api/catalogApi.ts:18:   return request<Course[]>(`https://api.codedev.test/courses${query}`, { signal });
src/features/catalog/hooks/usePopularCourses.ts:9:   fetch(`https://api.codedev.test/courses?category=${category}&sort=popular`)
src/features/catalog/components/RelatedCourses.tsx:31:   fetch(`https://api.codedev.test/courses?category=${course.category}&limit=4`)

Statement: "Changing how catalogApi.ts builds the query string is enough for every catalog request to send topic instead of category."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The grep shows two more places that build the URL themselves: a hook and, worse, a presentational component. Both keep sending ?category= after the API module is fixed, so the popular list and the related courses break on release day. An api/ folder only helps if it is the only code that knows URLs; the fix is to move both requests into catalogApi.ts (for example fetchCourses({ category, sort, limit })) and add a CI check that this grep matches only the API layer. The misconception is that having an API module means every call goes through it: the boundary exists only when something enforces it.', 1, 5
