WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_121'), 'The catalog state after a first refactor:

type CatalogState = {
  isLoading: boolean;
  isError: boolean;
  errorMessage: string | null;
  courses: Course[];
};

const [state, setState] = useState<CatalogState>({
  isLoading: false, isError: false, errorMessage: null, courses: [],
});

// in the search handler
setState((s) => ({ ...s, isLoading: true }));

Statement: "Because every field has a precise type, TypeScript rejects a state in which isLoading and isError are both true, or isError is true while errorMessage is null."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Each field is typed on its own, and nothing in the type relates them: two booleans, a nullable string and an array allow every combination, including loading and failed at once, or failed with no message. The spread in the search handler shows exactly how that happens: it sets isLoading and keeps whatever isError and courses were before. Only a union whose members carry their own fields, such as { kind: "loading" } | { kind: "error"; message: string } | ..., lets the compiler reject those combinations. The misconception is that precise field types give a precise state; they constrain each value, not how the values fit together.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
