WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_211'), 'The catalog reducer before the fix:

case "categoryToggled": {
  const categories = state.categories.includes(action.category)
    ? state.categories.filter((c) => c !== action.category)
    : [...state.categories, action.category];
  return { ...state, categories };
}

The learner was on page 7 of React, clicked the React chip off and the SQL chip on. Network tab:

GET https://api.codedev.test/courses?category=sql&page=7&pageSize=12
200 OK  {"items":[],"total":19,"totalPages":2}

Statement: "The API answered correctly for the request it received; the empty page comes from the reducer keeping page 7 when the categories changed."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The response is consistent: 19 SQL courses at 12 per page make 2 pages, so page 7 really is empty. The request asked for page 7 because the reducer copied the old page into the new state: `{ ...state, categories }` keeps every field it does not overwrite. A page number only means something for one combination of search, filters and sort, so every action that changes that combination must also set page to 1. The misconception is to blame the API (or to add a client-side "if empty, go back a page" patch) when the Network tab shows the client asked for a page that cannot exist.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_211') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', true),
        ('False', false)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_211'), 'After a refactor to useReducer, clicking "Next" does nothing visible:

function catalogReducer(state: CatalogState, action: CatalogAction): CatalogState {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "pageChanged":
      state.page = action.page;
      return state;
    // ...
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(catalogReducer, initialState);
<button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: "pageChanged", page: state.page + 1 })}>Next</button>

A console.log inside the reducer prints "page 2" after the click; the pager still shows "Page 1 of 5".

Statement: "The update is only delayed: dispatch is asynchronous, so the pager will show page 2 once React finishes processing the queued action."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. React did process the action - the log proves the reducer ran. It then compared the returned state with the current one using Object.is, got the same object (the reducer changed it in place and returned it), concluded nothing changed and skipped the re-render. Nothing is pending, so waiting does not help; the screen changes only if something else happens to re-render the component, and then it shows a value nobody asked React to render. The fix is to return a new object: `return { ...state, page: action.page }`. The misconception is that "asynchronous" explains every missing update; with reducers, a missing update usually means the same reference came back.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_211') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', false),
        ('False', true)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);

-- challenge chal_coding_trf_212 (mini_coding)
INSERT INTO challenge.challenges (public_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, name, title, description, status, difficulty, type, points, is_published, is_deleted)
VALUES ('chal_coding_trf_212', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'typescript-react-frontend'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'typescript-react-frontend') AND order_index = 2 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-trf-2-1' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'catalog-reducer', 'Reducer catalog: search, filters, sort and page in one place', 'The CodeDev catalog page keeps its search box, category chips, level filter, sort and page in one CatalogState, changed only by catalogReducer(state, action) through useReducer. Write the reducer. Every rule below that says "same state" means: return the state argument itself, not a copy. searchTyped: when text is a string different from state.search, set search to text exactly as typed and page to 1. categoryToggled: when category is one of CATEGORIES, remove it if present or append it at the end if not, and set page to 1. levelSet / sortChanged: when the value is one of LEVELS / SORTS and different from the current one, set it and page to 1. pageChanged: when page is an integer from 1 to state.totalPages and different from state.page, set it. filtersReset: search "", categories [], level "all", page 1, keeping sort and totalPages; same state when all four already have those values. resultsLoaded: when totalPages is an integer >= 0, set totalPages to max(1, totalPages) and page to min(page, totalPages); same state when neither value changes. Anything else - an unknown type, a missing field, or a value outside the types (the tests send actions parsed from the URL and from sessions saved by older versions of the app, such as page "5", page 2.5 or category "java") - returns the same state. Never change the state you receive or its categories array. Input (stdin): { "state": CatalogState, "actions": [...], "summary"?: true }. The test runner prints each action with the returned state, marked [same object] when you returned the state you received and [previous state mutated] when you changed it; with summary only the final state, how many actions returned the same object and how many mutated it.', 'open', 'medium'::difficulty_question, 'mini_coding', 30, true, false)
ON CONFLICT (public_id) DO UPDATE SET
    course_id = EXCLUDED.course_id, module_id = EXCLUDED.module_id, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, name = EXCLUDED.name,
    title = EXCLUDED.title, description = EXCLUDED.description, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, type = EXCLUDED.type,
    points = EXCLUDED.points, is_published = true, is_deleted = false, updated_at = now();
