Statement: "The input starts uncontrolled because `price` is undefined on the first render; starting the state with useState(\"\") makes it controlled from the first render and removes the warning."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. useState<string>() with no argument starts as undefined, and value={undefined} means "no value prop" to React, so the first render creates an uncontrolled input. The first keystroke sets a string, which makes it controlled, and React warns because an input should keep one mode for its whole life. With an empty string as the initial value, the input is controlled from the start. The misconception is that the warning is about onChange or about the type parameter; it is about the value being undefined on one render and a string on the next.', 1, 5
