WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_431'), 'SearchBox.tsx (no debounce yet):

useEffect(() => {
  if (!query) return;
  fetch(`https://api.codedev.test/courses?search=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`)
    .then((res) => res.json())
    .then(setResults);
}, [query]);

Network panel after typing "react" quickly (request, then time until the response finished):
?search=r      t=0 ms     110 ms
?search=re     t=60 ms    900 ms   <- finished last, at t=960
?search=rea    t=120 ms   130 ms
?search=reac   t=180 ms   120 ms
?search=react  t=240 ms   150 ms

The box shows "react", the list shows the results for "re".

Statement: "Returning a cleanup from this effect that aborts the request (an AbortController per run, controller.abort() in the cleanup) would keep the \"re\" response from replacing the \"react\" results."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. When query changes, React runs the previous effect''s cleanup before the next effect. Aborting there rejects the pending fetch for "re" with an AbortError, so its .then(setResults) never runs, whenever its bytes arrive. The fetch for "react" belongs to the last effect and is the only one allowed to set state. Abort does not stop the server from doing the work, and you still want a debounce so that four of these five requests are never sent; but for correctness the cleanup alone is enough. Verify it in the Network panel: the superseded requests show as (canceled). The misconception is that the order of responses follows the order of requests.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_trf_431') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
